+# Funding metal0's operator C9 through standard EIP-7702 account maintenance: source-only assessment
+
+Written 2026-10-23T23:50Z.
+
+This is an assessment only. There were no keys, RPC calls, SSH, signing, broadcasts or mutations, and no tool is
+implemented. Root reviews it before deciding anything or asking a human. **This document does not claim permission to
+do any of it.**
+
+## Facts this rests on (root evidence, read only)
+
+- **Operator C9.** C9 = `0xc9d0835cd8ebc7eae84304d9db0a76253eebe6ab`.
+  - `setProofKey` mined at nonce 23759, block 53004641 (`custody-v2-live-20261023/evidence/registration-receipt-2341.json`).
+  - Now: nonce **23760**, balance **145,368,427,115 wei**.
+  - The gate failed and was rolled back at 23:43:19Z (`custody-boot-fee-activation-20261023/result.json`). The previous
+    `setProofKey` worst case was 271,929,253,610 wei.
+- **Delegation.** C9's code is the 7702 designator `0xef0100‖0x8900baca2167873ba2f8a8bae8b4876e6396d343`, codehash
+  `0xe2f1317f…bbe`; the executor runtime is `0xdd893cd4…`.
+- **Why plain ETH fails.** The executor (`EnclaveLeaseExecutor.sol`) has only nonpayable functions and no
+  `receive`/`fallback`, so a plain ETH send to C9 reverts.
+
+## The option, and how it differs from the rejected route
+
+| | rejected (do not retry) | this option |
+|---|---|---|
+| mechanism | a new helper contract that SELFDESTRUCTs ETH into C9 (forced funding) | the protocol's own 7702 revocation (authorization to address 0), a plain value transfer, then re-delegation to the SAME reviewed executor |
+| new code on chain | yes | **none**; no contract, no admin change, no generic execute path |
+| C9's delegation | untouched | **temporarily removed** (one-block-scale window), then restored byte-exactly |
+| needs C9's key | no | **yes**: two authorization signatures |
+
+It changes C9's account state twice. That needs explicit approval of its own; it is not covered by any earlier approval.
+
+## The two transactions
+
+The sponsor S is an already-funded EOA, not C9. N = C9's nonce at signing (23760 if nothing moves it).
+
+| tx | sender / nonce | authorizationList (signed by C9's key) | call | effect |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| A | S / s | `{chainId: 8453, address: 0x0000…0000, nonce: N}` | `to: C9, value: V, data: 0x` | before execution: C9's code is cleared (empty hash) and its nonce goes to N+1. Execution: a plain transfer of V to a codeless account. |
+| B | S / s+1 | `{chainId: 8453, address: 0x8900baca…d343, nonce: N+1}` | `to: S, value: 0, data: 0x` (never into C9) | C9's code is again `0xef0100‖executor` (codehash `e2f1317f`), nonce N+2 |
+
+Why two transactions: every authorization is applied BEFORE execution. Clear-and-restore in ONE transaction would leave
+the executor in place when the value arrives, and the transfer would revert.
+
+## Is it supported? (local sources only)
+
+**EIP-7702 processing** — `revm-handler 43.0.3 src/pre_execution.rs::apply_auth_list`, the EIP's numbered steps
+inline; `revm-context-interface 43.0.2 journaled_state/account.rs::delegate`:
+- **Address 0:** "do not write the designation. Clear the account's code and reset the code hash to the empty hash".
+- **Every applied authorization** bumps the authority's nonce.
+- **`delegate()` writes only code and nonce.** Storage and balance are untouched.
+- **An invalid authorization** (wrong chain id, nonce ≠ the account's nonce, or a bad signature) is skipped with
+  `continue`: the transaction still executes.
+
+**viem 2.53.1** (`actions/wallet/prepareAuthorization.js`; the repo's own lockfile copy):
+- **The address is free:** any address is accepted, including 0.
+- **Nonce rule:** the nonce defaults to the authority's PENDING nonce, plus 1 only when the authority itself sends the
+  transaction. Here the sponsor sends, so A uses N and B uses N+1.
+- **Sending:** `sendTransaction({ authorizationList })` exists.
+- **Precedent in this codebase:** `relay/host-sponsor.mjs` sends a relayer-paid type-4 transaction carrying an
+  operator-signed authorization on Base. It is a different target (Simple7702Account), but the same mechanism.
+
+**Base:** C9 itself is already 7702-delegated on Base, so Base processes type-4 transactions.
+
+**The contracts** (`history-checkpoint-transport/tree/contracts`):
+- **The reserve's check:** `EnclaveLeaseGasReserve.begin` requires `extcodehash(operator) == delegationCodeHash =
+  keccak256(0xef0100‖executor)`. So B must restore exactly `0x8900baca…`; any other target leaves the leases refused.
+- **The executor's state:** it keeps each deployment's lease nonce in C9's own storage under an ERC-7201 namespace.
+  The source says this is so that re-delegation cannot reset them. Its "first authorization charged" flag is in C9's
+  storage, keyed by the executor's address.
+
+**Not verified here:**
+- that the deployed executor runtime `dd893cd4…` equals this source (not rebuilt);
+- the Base sequencer's txpool rules for type-4 transactions (for example, limits on authorities with pending
+  transactions);
+- the exact revm parity of the client version Base runs;
+- whether MetaMask accepts a dapp-supplied `authorizationList`. This was not checked; assume no.
+
+## What is preserved
+
+- **Lease nonces and the executor's flags:** preserved. Storage is never touched, and the same executor reads the same
+  slots after B, so lease nonces continue and the "first authorization" flag stays set (no re-charge).
+- **C9's account nonce:** goes up by 2 (N → N+2). Lease signatures do not use it. Any already-signed C9 EOA
+  transaction at N or N+1 becomes invalid; none may exist.
+- **Balance:** C9 gains exactly V. Nothing else moves.
+- **The reserve's binding:** `delegationCodeHash` is unchanged, so after B the reserve accepts C9 again.
+
+## What must be quiet first
+
+1. **Nothing that can sign as C9 is running.** That means the control unit, boot/funding tools and keepers; root's
+   rollback state already has the new control stopped.
+   - This matters for two reasons. Any C9 transaction moves N and silently invalidates A or B. And a lease client that
+     attaches its own fresh authorization would race B (not verified for metal0's client).
+2. **No outstanding sponsored Lease.** Every C9-signed Lease deadline must have passed on a verified block; the TTL is
+   300 s, as in `safe_rollback`.
+   - This is the main hazard. While C9 has no code, a relayer's `lease()` call to C9 runs against an empty account and
+     **succeeds as a no-op**: no revert, no renewal, no reserve charge. A relayer could record a renewal that never
+     happened.
+3. **Live leases held by C9:** the two guests in `result.json` (holder `0xc9d0…`). They get no renewals while the node
+   is quiet. Their fresh expiries must exceed the quiet period, plus the A→B window, plus a margin; otherwise root
+   accepts or prevents the lapse first.
+4. **Nonces settled:**
+   - C9: latest == pending on two providers, equal to N.
+   - S: settled at s, with balance ≥ V + bounded fees for A and B (type-4 L1 size includes the authorization tuples;
+     reuse the reviewed fee rule).
+
+## Interruption cases
+
+| case | outcome | recovery |
+|---|---|---|
+| A's authorization invalid (N moved, bad signature) | skipped; the call hits the executor and reverts; V stays with S; S pays gas | none needed; re-plan |
+| A mined, B delayed or dropped | C9 is a funded plain EOA (funds safe: only C9's key moves them). The reserve refuses it, and relayed `lease()` calls are silent no-ops. | rebroadcast the same B (valid while C9's nonce = N+1). Or, since C9 is now funded, C9 self-sends a restore (authorization nonce = current + 1), which needs a fresh C9 signature. |
+| C9 sends anything between A and B | the presigned B is invalid | a fresh restore authorization, as above |
+| a third party copies A's authorization into its own transaction | C9 cleared but unfunded; our A reverts (stale authorization) | send B (now valid). This is griefing only, with no loss; Base's sequencer mempool is not a public p2p pool (not verified for this setup). |
+| a chain-id-0 authorization | could be replayed on any chain where C9's nonce matches | **never**: chainId must be 8453 |
+| a reorg | both transactions can be re-included as the same signed bytes | verify only after 2 confirmations on two providers |
+| B leaked early | harmless: valid only at N+1, and it only restores the reviewed executor | — |
+
+## A finite plan (no implementation yet)
+
+**0. Approvals** (root first, then each human role).
+- **C9 key holder.** The key is root-only (`metal/config.json` registryKey). They approve signing exactly two
+  authorizations: `{8453, 0x0, N}` and `{8453, 0x8900baca…, N+1}`.
+- **Funding owner.** They approve V and the sponsor:
+  - V's size is root's decision, for example "the next setProofKey/enrollment worst case plus margin" (271,929,253,610
+    wei was the previous worst case).
+  - S should be a dedicated EOA, funded by a plain transfer, that can sign raw type-4 transactions (not MetaMask).
+- **Leases.** Root accepts the lease plan from item 3 above.
+
+**1. Read-only preflight.** Two providers, one agreed block. It confirms:
+- C9's code equals the designator (codehash `e2f1317f`), with latest = pending = N, and C9's balance;
+- the executor code hash `dd893cd4…` and the reserve's `delegationCodeHash` `e2f1317f`;
+- no live Lease deadline, the live guests' lease expiries, and `nonceOf(id)` for their deployments;
+- S's nonce and balance;
+- an `eth_call` simulation of A and of B with their authorization lists;
+- the fee bounds (L2 cap + L1 upper bound for the signed type-4 size + Isthmus operator fee) against caps.
+
+**2. Sign.**
+- The C9 key holder signs the two authorizations, and S signs A (s) and B (s+1).
+- Both raw transactions are journaled privately (0600, fsynced) before any broadcast, as with the reviewed
+  journal-before-send pattern.
+
+**3. Broadcast** A, then B immediately, from the same sender in nonce order.
+- **After A:** C9's code is empty, nonce N+1, balance + V.
+- **After B:** the designator is back, codehash `e2f1317f`, nonce N+2.
+- **Unchanged throughout:** `nonceOf(id)`, `authorizationCharged()` and the reserve binding, each at 2 confirmations on
+  both providers.
+
+**4. Resume** the control and leases only after B is verified.
+
+There is no rollback for the funds. The only open state is "A done, B not", and its recovery is in the table above.
